  "Question": "A fair coin is tossed \\(3\\) times. What is the probability of at least two consecutive heads?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Write \\(H\\) for heads and \\(T\\) for tails. The three fair tosses give eight equally likely ordered sequences:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&HHH,\\ HHT,\\ HTH,\\ HTT,\\\\\n&THH,\\ THT,\\ TTH,\\ TTT.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis list includes both possibilities at each position, with no sequence repeated.\n\nA consecutive pair of heads must occupy either the first two tosses or the last two tosses. Exactly \\(HHH\\), \\(HHT\\), and \\(THH\\) qualify. We count \\(HHH\\) once, even though it contains two adjacent pairs of heads. Thus the probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{8},\n\\]\nchoice C.",
